The culprit appears to be the `.utcOffset()` function 

```js
// Environment:
//   dayjs v1.11.13 (with UTC plugin)
//   Node v22.17.0 or Deno 2.2.11 -- Both with the exact same error
//   Windows 11 (TZ = UTC–03:00)
```


```js
dayjs.utc('2000-01-01T06:01:02Z').valueOf()
946706462000 // Looks ok

dayjs.utc('2000-01-01T06:01:02Z').utcOffset(1, true).valueOf()
946695662000 // Looks odd

// Difference equals my local tz offset (-3 hours)
```

------------------------------

This next one is kind of scary because it breaks *dayjs* immutability:

```js
const date = dayjs.utc('2000-01-01T06:01:02Z');

date.valueOf();
946706462000

date.utcOffset(1, true).valueOf();
946695662000

date.valueOf();
946695662000 // Different from the previous date.valueOf()

// Basically if `.utcOffset()` is called, it mutates the date variable 
// in a way that it renders future data manipulation unreliable
```


